# libwacom -- the tablet database mutter and the Wacom panel read.
#
# It does not talk to hardware. It is a database plus a lookup library: given a
# device, it answers what the tablet IS -- how many buttons, whether it has a
# touch ring, which stylus types it takes, the physical size used to map it to
# a screen. mutter needs it to map a tablet to a monitor, and
# gnome-control-center's Wacom panel is a front end for the same answers.
# Without it a tablet still works as a pointer and stops being configurable.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
# BLFS 12.4 pins 2.16.1.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/linuxwacom/libwacom/releases/download/libwacom-2.16.1/libwacom-2.16.1.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=0f9bc90babad92b2c4c6571b53af3aee065f437cce01c06c860599e1a10680aa
SRC_DIR=libwacom-2.16.1

# ==============================================================================
# -Dtests=disabled, AND IT IS REQUIRED RATHER THAN TIDY
# ==============================================================================
# The option is a feature whose value is 'enabled' -- not auto, ENABLED -- so
# the default builds the test suite, and the test block is not merely compiled.
# Inside `if get_option('tests').enabled()`, guarded only by b_sanitize being
# 'none' (which is the default), meson.build:314 runs
#
#     pymod.find_installation(modules: ['libevdev', 'pyudev', 'pytest'])
#     pytest = find_program('pytest-3', 'pytest')
#
# NEITHER HAS required: false. find_installation with a modules list is
# required by default, and so is find_program. python-libevdev, pyudev and
# pytest are none of them packaged -- Duct's python is built
# --without-ensurepip and has no third-party modules at all -- so the upstream
# default FAILS AT CONFIGURE here, before anything is compiled.
#
# This is the third instance of the same shape in this chain: an option whose
# default is on, reaching for a program or module that is not packaged, with
# no soft-failure path.
#
# ==============================================================================
# udev-dir: NOT PASSED, AND CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED
# ==============================================================================
# libwacom installs a udev rules generator and its output, so the directory
# matters for the same reason it did in upower -- rules nothing reads are rules
# that do not classify a device. Here the default is already right:
#
#     dir_sys_udev = get_option('prefix') / 'lib' / 'udev'      (meson.build:13)
#     dir_udev = get_option('udev-dir'); if '' then dir_sys_udev
#
# With prefix=/usr that is /usr/lib/udev, which is where eudev actually keeps
# its rules (its recipe passes --libexecdir=/usr/lib). So unlike upower, whose
# 'auto' resolved through a dependency() on a .pc file, this one is computed
# from the prefix and cannot drift. No flag is passed; install.sh asserts the
# rules landed there anyway, because that is the outcome and the derivation is
# only an argument about it.
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE REST
# ==============================================================================
# -Ddocumentation=disabled. ALREADY THE DEFAULT VALUE, and passed anyway
# because the option's own description contradicts it: it reads
# "[default=auto]" while the value is 'disabled'. Reading the prose gives the
# wrong answer and reading the value gives the right one. It needs doxygen,
# which is not packaged, so if that description were ever made true the build
# would start failing. Naming it costs nothing and pins the side that works.
#
# NOTE ON python: meson.build:22 calls
# `pymod.find_installation('python3', required: true)` UNCONDITIONALLY -- not
# inside the tests block. python is a hard build dependency of this package
# even with tests disabled, and it is declared as one.
MESON_ARGS="-Dtests=disabled -Ddocumentation=disabled"
